.TH VERBA 1 "2026-11-02" "verba 0.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
verba \- audit what a command says against what it does
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B verba
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B verba
runs
.I command
under
.BR ptrace (2),
watching both sides of the conversation \(em its output and exit code on one
side, the file syscalls it actually makes on the other \(em and reports every
contradiction between the two.
.PP
Three contradiction classes are reported:
.TP
.B faithless claim
The output claims a write to a path that no traced process ever opened for
writing.
.TP
.B undeclared write
A file was created, modified or removed on disk, but no output line mentions it.
Writes under
.IR /tmp ,
.IR /dev ,
.IR /proc ,
.IR /sys ,
.IR /run ,
.IR $TMPDIR ,
and to non-files (pipes, sockets) are ignored; extend the set with
.BR \-\-allow .
.TP
.B discourse mismatch
The output reports success while the command failed (exit status other than 0),
or reports an error while the command exited 0.
.TP
.BR mistranslation " (heuristic)"
The error wording disagrees with the real
.IR errno :
the output blames "permission denied" while the call actually failed with
.BR ENOENT ,
or claims a write that in fact failed. This class is a heuristic (phase 2),
matched against a built-in
.I errno
dictionary, and is only computed with
.B \-\-heuristic
or
.BR \-\-strict .
.PP
.B verba
is pure C with no runtime dependencies and targets x86-64.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write the JSON report to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the JSON report to
.IR FILE ;
the human-readable summary is still printed to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-\-allow " " \fIPREFIX\fR
Treat writes under
.I PREFIX
as declared. May be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-tty
Run the command on a pty instead of pipes, for programs that change what they
say when stdout is not a terminal. stdout and stderr are then captured as one
combined stream (reported as \"tty\" in JSON).
.TP
.BR \-\-follow " " \fIPID\fR
Attach to an already running process instead of launching a command, with
.BR PTRACE_SEIZE (2).
Its stdout and stderr cannot be redirected after the fact, so the words are
reconstructed from the
.BR write (2)
calls it makes to file descriptors 1 and 2. Only the target and the descendants
it spawns from the moment of attachment are followed; pre-existing children and
threads are not. Cannot be combined with a command or with
.BR \-\-tty .
.TP
.B \-\-heuristic
Also run the phase-2 mistranslation checks
.RI ( errno
versus claim). The findings are reported in a separate block and do
.I not
affect the exit status.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Run the heuristic checks and count their findings toward the exit status.
.TP
.BR \-\-patterns " " \fIFILE\fR
Load extra errno-to-phrase patterns from
.IR FILE ,
a JSON array of {"phrase", "errno"} objects, used by the mistranslation checks.
May be given more than once.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Faithful: the command ran and no contradictions were found.
.TP
.B 1
Contradictions were found between words and syscalls.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error or verba-internal failure.
.PP
The traced command's own exit status is reported in the output, not propagated
as the exit status of
.BR verba .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR strace (1),
.BR ptrace (2)
.SH AUTHOR
Elena Frison.
